WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.540
- So pretend you're this guy,

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:04.710
and you're in bed typing
in random country names

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.260
on Google Flights, checking the prices

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.360
because, you know, after
the pandemic is over

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.870
you're gonna travel the world

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:14.560
and see and taste things you've
never seen or tasted before.

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:15.812
But then you remember...

00:00:15.812 --> 00:00:17.150
(laughs) I'm broke.

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:18.810
If you've ever been in this situation,

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.080
then you're not alone.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:21.370
Throughout most of my life,

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:24.740
I always found that no matter
how much money I made per hour

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:26.620
in any of the weird jobs I worked,

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:28.930
by the end of the month
I was always broke.

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:30.660
It's like somebody had a giant tube

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.310
connected to my bank account

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.860
and was just siphoning
money away gradually

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.140
until by the week before payday,

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.090
I was living on ramen and eggs.

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:42.730
But I noticed something,
the reason I was broke

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:45.880
wasn't because there was
some third-party involved.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:47.020
The problem was me.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:49.010
And I was succumbing and falling prey

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:52.370
to the three most common money mistakes

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:53.960
that most people make.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.130
And unless you correct these three things

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.890
you'll probably end up in
that exact same situation.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:00.840
But if you do correct the mistakes,

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.830
then you may find that
at the end of the month,

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:04.150
you'll just be a little bit,

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:06.540
you know, your wallet will
be a little bit fatter

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.830
and your tummy just a
little bit more full.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:10.730
I said, tummy again. I hate that word.

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:12.210
Okay, mistake number one,

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:14.650
you're succumbing to lifestyle inflation.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:18.300
Lifestyle inflation is when
you start making more money

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.060
and then as a result, you
start spending more money.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.297
You think to yourself,
"Sick, I just got a raise.

00:01:23.297 --> 00:01:25.817
"I'm making an extra $300 every month.

00:01:25.817 --> 00:01:28.577
"Now I can probably afford
a Disney+ subscription,

00:01:28.577 --> 00:01:30.687
"I can buy those Nike Air Force 1s,

00:01:30.687 --> 00:01:33.120
"and I can hit the pub
tonight with Jim and Rand."

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.470
Lifestyle inflation, in essence,

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:37.467
comes from the weird fallacy that,

00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:39.817
"Okay, rich people have nice things.

00:01:39.817 --> 00:01:41.867
"And since I'm making
a little bit more money

00:01:41.867 --> 00:01:43.807
"and getting a little
bit more rich myself,

00:01:43.807 --> 00:01:45.680
"then I should start buying nice things

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:46.540
like the rich people."

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:49.670
But in reality, somewhere deep
in your subconscious mind,

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:52.020
you know that the reason people are rich

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.860
is because they have money.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:56.810
And when you spend money,
you no longer have money.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:58.590
But you probably already know this.

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:00.210
I'm not saying anything new.

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:02.160
So how do we solve this problem?

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.470
Well, first of all,
it's an awareness thing.

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:05.940
We have to be aware of this thing

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:07.540
called the hedonic treadmill.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:09.670
The hedonic treadmill is a metaphor

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:13.010
for the idea that an
individual's level of happiness

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:16.480
tends to return to where
it started, a set point,

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:18.250
regardless of good fortune

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:20.970
or negative life events
the person experiences.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:23.610
So in essence, you can
go balls to the wall

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:25.870
and spend money on the most lavish

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:28.180
and ridiculous things in life.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:30.420
You know, if you get millions and millions

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:31.580
and billions of dollars,

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:34.500
you will always be able to find
things to spend the money on

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.250
and those things can be
really shiny and really cool.

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:40.260
But the fact of the matter
is, within a couple of days,

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.280
couple of weeks, you're just gonna return

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:43.890
to that baseline happiness.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.450
So if you wanna fix
your lifestyle inflation

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.920
and your incessant need
to buy things all the time

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:52.370
to make you happy, first be
aware of the hedonic treadmill.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:56.200
And the next thing to do is
to fix mistake number two,

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:59.360
which is you don't pay yourself first.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.540
So, since you're super
efficient at spending

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:04.900
every little nickel in your
bank account with Jim and Rand,

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:07.560
you need to create a safeguard mechanism

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.340
that protects you from doing that.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:10.700
Something that has your back

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:13.560
even in your weakest, most primal moments.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.780
And that is to pay yourself first.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.470
Every single month you
pay the internet company,

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:20.280
the phone plan company.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.560
You pay Disney. You pay Apple.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.530
You're handing out your
hard-earned cash likes it's...

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:30.570
Like it's a free money day. (laughs)

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:32.320
You're paying all these other people

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:35.150
but you can never get rich
if you don't pay yourself.

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:36.970
Give yourself some of your own income.

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:38.640
Why does everyone else have to take it?

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:39.820
Pretty much everyone,

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:42.350
regardless of their financial situation.

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:46.210
can afford to live on 90%
of their monthly income.

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:48.040
Like, if you learned next month,

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:52.140
that instead of making
$3,000, for instance,

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.650
you'd be making only $2,700,

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:56.550
you'd find a way to make it work.

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:58.050
You'd stop buying lattes.

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:00.669
You would cut corners on certain things.

00:04:00.669 --> 00:04:01.806
(camera scrapes)

00:04:01.806 --> 00:04:02.639
No!

00:04:02.639 --> 00:04:04.500
It happens to a lot of
people all the time.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.870
They find out they're not gonna
be making as much next month

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:08.640
or they're not gonna be
getting as many shifts.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.270
They make it work. They survive.

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:12.450
But get in the habit
of treating your income

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:14.530
like it's a 90% thing.

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:18.230
90% you can spend on whatever
else you wanna spend it on,

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:20.960
but that 10% you give to yourself.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:22.630
You put it away and you save it.

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:24.540
We can talk about
investing in another video.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:28.070
But if you're not already
saving 10% of what you make,

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:30.900
as a minimum, your net worth isn't growing

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.460
and you're not improving
your situation ever.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:35.540
Just briefly though, if you have debt,

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:37.590
which is a lot of you watching probably.

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:39.560
I've been there. It's called college.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:41.800
One of the best techniques
that I've heard of

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:44.120
is the debt snowball tactic,

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:46.060
which was developed by Dave Ramsey.

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:47.860
And that's basically just the idea

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:51.230
of paying off your smaller chunks first.

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:54.330
So your smaller loans, maybe
it's a small credit card.

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:55.887
Then you move up to
your bigger credit card

00:04:55.887 --> 00:04:58.290
and you move up to your car
and then your student loans.

00:04:58.290 --> 00:04:59.970
And this kind of goes against the notion

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:01.350
that you have to tackle the debts

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:03.520
with the highest interest rates first.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:05.350
And while that's mathematically true,

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:06.940
a lot of people just don't do it.

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:10.090
They didn't get into debt because
they were smart with math.

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:11.780
They got into debt because

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:13.990
they make weak financial decisions

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:15.680
based on impulse and emotion.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:18.590
So you need to get impulse
and emotion on your side.

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:22.070
It's really motivating to pay
off your small debts first.

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:24.270
It gives you momentum
and it gets you stoked

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:25.770
to pay off your bigger debts.

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:27.080
You can move on to your other debts

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:29.970
and then you can use the
interest that you were spending

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:31.757
on the smaller debts to
pay off your bigger debts.

00:05:31.757 --> 00:05:33.630
And you create this debt snowball

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:36.240
where you're just used to
paying off debts all the time.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:37.073
And it's great.

00:05:37.073 --> 00:05:38.200
But you can't do any of that

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:40.870
if you're just paying the
minimum payments on everything

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:44.110
and then spending the rest
of the money everywhere else.

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:47.720
Take that 10% and use it
to improve your situation.

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:49.490
Okay, so mistake number three

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:52.000
is that you're falling for consumer traps.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:54.340
Now, these can be extremely insidious.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:56.840
So have you ever been
going about your day,

00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:59.460
you know, you went to work
and then you went to the gym

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:00.890
like a good strong boy?

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:02.060
You know, and then you get home

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:04.360
and you start browsing the computer.

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:05.420
And you're on YouTube

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:07.867
and your favorite tech reviewer says,

00:06:07.867 --> 00:06:10.770
"The all-new iPad. 10.-whatever-inch."

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:12.347
And at the end of the video, he says,

00:06:12.347 --> 00:06:14.427
"Now is the time to buy an iPad."

00:06:14.427 --> 00:06:16.637
"This is a great iPad.

00:06:16.637 --> 00:06:18.607
"Lots of improvements over the last one.

00:06:18.607 --> 00:06:20.150
"You should go ahead and buy it now."

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:20.983
So sure enough,

00:06:20.983 --> 00:06:23.790
you go on to Google and
you type in Apple iPad.

00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:25.400
And you add it to your cart.

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:26.507
And you think to yourself,

00:06:26.507 --> 00:06:29.527
"I actually have $300 more
than I thought I would.

00:06:29.527 --> 00:06:32.600
"And this iPad is only
$300. Let's get it done."

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:34.140
So you add it your cart, you buy it,

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:35.830
and you pretend you just
didn't even do anything.

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:37.160
You walked away from the computer.

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:39.800
Try to pretend that you didn't
actually just buy an iPad.

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:41.800
You ignore the negative thoughts

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:43.270
and you just focus on the positive.

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:44.920
You're like, "Ah, that
was a nice thing to do."

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:47.250
But here's the weird thing that
not very many people notice

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:49.310
about that kind of situation.

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:51.270
You went your entire day

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:53.830
without even thinking
about an iPad, you know?

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:55.350
You were just living your life,

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:57.230
unaffected by this iPad,

00:06:57.230 --> 00:06:59.510
totally content without needing an iPad.

00:06:59.510 --> 00:07:01.870
And had you not watched
that YouTube video,

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:03.380
you'd have had a great night's sleep

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:05.170
and you would have been $300 richer.

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:07.060
But because of how marketing works,

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:10.010
all of a sudden when you come
across the ad for the iPad

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:11.870
or something promoting the iPad,

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:13.490
it becomes the most important

00:07:13.490 --> 00:07:16.550
and tempting thing in your entire world.

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:20.470
You have this deep sense of
lack about not having this thing

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:23.750
and buying this thing would
really just put that to rest.

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:26.110
That's marketing. That's a consumer trap.

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:27.610
It's like a bunch of little Fruit Loops

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:30.010
on a trail leading you to spending money.

00:07:30.010 --> 00:07:32.470
In my own life, the biggest consumer trap

00:07:32.470 --> 00:07:35.540
in my entire existence
is camera equipment.

00:07:35.540 --> 00:07:37.790
And my brain has this rationalization

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:40.747
that, "Oh, I'm a YouTuber and
I'm really into video stuff.

00:07:40.747 --> 00:07:43.060
"So I gotta stay on top
of the latest tech."

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:46.460
But every time I stay in
this consumerist mentality,

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:48.150
I produce way less.

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:50.750
And if I've learned anything
about being a YouTuber,

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:52.440
is that in order to succeed

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:54.920
I need to produce a lot
more than I consume.

00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:56.860
That's kind of the way it goes in life.

00:07:56.860 --> 00:07:59.650
If you're constantly on
social media, on YouTube,

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:02.960
and you've subscribed to all
these like equipment reviews

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:05.610
or yacht club catalog, whatever,

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:07.580
it's basically just a consumer trap.

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:09.490
It's a gateway into spending money.

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:11.410
It's a gateway to giving away your money

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:13.760
to everyone else other than yourself.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:16.870
So the solution is to
start deleting the traps.

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:19.160
You know, my favorite camera reviewers,

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:21.097
I had to click on YouTube and say,

00:08:21.097 --> 00:08:23.170
"Don't recommend this kind of shit to me."

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:24.080
It's heartbreaking,

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:26.630
but you gotta eliminate
these traps as you see them.

00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:28.640
In "Atomic Habits" by James Clear,

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:30.740
he talks about how willpower

00:08:30.740 --> 00:08:32.970
is kind of for losers, in a way.

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:36.920
Like most people have
optimized their existence

00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:39.750
and their homes and their online life

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:42.360
so that they don't have to
use willpower all the time.

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:44.920
They don't have to sit
there resisting urges.

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:46.700
They've just deleted it ahead of time.

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:48.280
They've gotten rid of the traps

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:51.710
so that they can just go about
their business undisturbed

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:53.420
without all these
companies just kind of like

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:56.580
tugging on you to go spend
money on their stuff.

00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:59.440
So don't succumb to lifestyle inflation,

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:03.440
pay yourself first, and then
eliminate these consumer traps.

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:05.130
And you'll be so much better off.

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:07.780
You'll have so much more
extra money lying around.

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:10.350
And if you wanna learn
more about personal finance

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:14.780
without getting into like the
crazy mind-numbing numbers,

00:09:14.780 --> 00:09:17.400
talking about Roth IRAs and 401ks

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:19.490
and investments and all of that,

